"""
Dirichlet Stick-Breaking Process — how many groups are in my data, when I don't know
the number in advance?

WHAT IT DOES: You're clustering customers, or finding topics in documents. K-means
demands you pick K first. The Dirichlet Process says: don't pick — let the data
decide, keep a bit of probability aside for "a group I haven't seen yet." Stick-breaking
is HOW you build that: imagine a stick of length 1 (= 100% of probability). Break off
a random piece — that's group 1's share. Break a piece of the REMAINDER — group 2's
share. Keep going. α (concentration) controls the breaking style: small α → a few
dominant groups; large α → many small groups.

WHEN TO USE: You're doing clustering or topic modeling and don't know the number of
clusters in advance. The stick-breaking process is the engine behind infinite mixture
models and Latent Dirichlet Allocation.

GUARANTEES: Weights sum to approximately 1 (converges as num_breaks → ∞). Higher α
produces more active clusters (E[K(α)] increasing in α). The sum of weights exceeds
0.9 for α=1.0 with 100 breaks.

NOT: Neural-network weights (the name misleads). These are probability shares of
clusters/topics in Bayesian nonparametrics. The "sums to ≈1" promise breaks when
α ≫ num_breaks (AURA-E011).
"""
